    <!-- =========================
         MAIN EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example: Employees Above Department Average
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose we want to find employees whose salary is greater
                than the average salary of their own department.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT
    e.EmployeeName,
    e.DepartmentId,
    e.Salary
FROM Employees e
WHERE e.Salary &gt;
(
    SELECT AVG(e2.Salary)
    FROM Employees e2
    WHERE e2.DepartmentId = e.DepartmentId
);
            </code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         HOW IT WORKS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                How Does a Correlated Subquery Work?
            </h2>

            <ol>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    The outer query selects an employee.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    The inner query finds the average salary for that
                    employee's department.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    The inner query uses
                    <code>e.DepartmentId</code> from the outer query.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    The employee's salary is compared with the calculated
                    department average.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    The process is repeated for the other employees.
                </li>

            </ol>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         CORRELATED vs NORMAL
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Normal Subquery vs Correlated Subquery
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Normal Subquery</th>
                            <th>Correlated Subquery</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Can generally execute independently.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Depends on the outer query.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Does not normally reference the outer
                                query.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                References a column from the outer query.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Generally evaluated independently of
                                individual outer rows.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Evaluated in relation to rows of the
                                outer query.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         ANOTHER EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example: Highest Paid Employee in Each Department
            </h2>

            <p>
                The following correlated subquery finds employees whose
                salary is equal to the maximum salary in their department.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT
    e.EmployeeName,
    e.DepartmentId,
    e.Salary
FROM Employees e
WHERE e.Salary =
(
    SELECT MAX(e2.Salary)
    FROM Employees e2
    WHERE e2.DepartmentId = e.DepartmentId
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The inner query calculates the maximum salary for the
                department of the current employee.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li>
                A correlated subquery depends on the outer query.
            </li>

            <li>
                The inner query references a column from the outer query.
            </li>

            <li>
                Table aliases make correlated subqueries easier to read.
            </li>

            <li>
                Correlated subqueries are useful for row-by-row comparisons.
            </li>

            <li>
                They can be used with aggregate functions such as
                AVG(), MAX() and MIN().
            </li>

            <li>
                For large datasets, query performance should be considered
                when using correlated subqueries.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-info p-4 mb-4">

        <h2 class="fw-bold">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p class="mb-0">
            A correlated subquery is a subquery that depends on the
            outer query. It references a column from the outer query
            and is evaluated in relation to the rows being processed.
            It is especially useful for comparing each row with
            aggregated or related data from the same group.
        </p>

    </div>


